{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_73jpghdswswjnmt7mmnc6o6u22qut5pmvvmszhtp33j5zwwrlqra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01XEET3dQyMQepQLsSxrJjeS","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0NMgaL/translated_rust/VERIFICATION.md","content":"# VERIFICATION.md — differential verification of the C → Rust translation\n\n`c_src/src/lib.c` (a slice of cute_c2 / tinyc2's GJK) is the ground truth.\n`src/lib.rs` must behave identically through the FFI boundary.\n\nReproduce everything with:\n\n```sh\n./run_all.sh\n```\n\nIt enumerates the build configurations, builds the C `.so` and the Rust cdylib\n(dev **and** release), checks `nm -D` symbol parity for both, and runs the whole\ndifferential suite against both. Current result:\n\n```\n=== Enumerate build configurations ===\n  no [features] in Cargo.toml -> 1 combination: <default>\n=== Symbol parity (nm -D) ===\n  PASS debug:   all 31 C symbols exported by Rust\n  PASS debug:   no non-libc undefined symbols\n  PASS release: all 31 C symbols exported by Rust\n  PASS release: no non-libc undefined symbols\n=== Differential test suite ===\n  PASS features=<default> rust_so=dev     (88 tests)\n  PASS features=<default> rust_so=release (88 tests)\nALL CHECKS PASSED\n```\n\n## How the tests are wired\n\nBoth libraries are loaded with `libloading` and every symbol is resolved as a\nraw `extern \"C\"` function pointer (`tests/common/mod.rs`). **No test ever calls\nthe Rust crate directly**, so the `#[no_mangle]` export wrappers and the ABI\n(struct layout, by-value `c2v` returns in XMM0, `C2_TYPE` as `int`) are part of\nwhat is under test. Inputs are generated by a seeded xorshift64\\* PRNG, so every\nrun is reproducible.\n\n| file | scope |\n|------|-------|\n| `tests/smoke.rs` | harness self-check, layout assertions |\n| `tests/vectors.rs` | CONFIGS rows 1–19 — vector helpers, proxy construction |\n| `tests/simplex.rs` | CONFIGS rows 20–27 — `c22`/`c23`/`c2D`/`c2L`/`c2Witness`/`c2Support` driven directly |\n| `tests/gjk.rs` | CONFIGS rows 28–50 — the full `c2GJK` pipeline, incl. multi-call cached sequences |\n| `tests/public.rs` | CONFIGS rows 51–56 — `gjk_cache`, the only entry point in `lib.h` |\n| `tests/errors.rs` | ERRORS rows 1–60 — one test per rejection, plus subprocess trap-parity |\n\nPhase A artifacts: `SYMBOLS.md` (31/31 symbols), `ERRORS.md` (60 rows + 35b),\n`CONFIGS.md` (56 rows, 1 build configuration).\n\n## Comparison policy\n\n* **Strict (bit-exact)** for every NaN-free input — including `±0`, `±inf`,\n  subnormals, `±FLT_MAX`, and **hardware-generated NaNs**. An invalid operation\n  (`inf*0`, `0/0`, `inf-inf`) yields the x86 indefinite `0xffc00000` in both\n  languages because both emit the same SSE instruction, so those are covered\n  strictly too.\n* **NaN-payload-insensitive** only when an *input* already contains a NaN.\n  `addss dst, src` returns the *destination* operand when both operands are NaN,\n  and which product lands in the destination register is a register-allocation\n  choice that differs between gcc -O0 and LLVM (shown from the disassembly in\n  `ERRORS.md`). Recompiling the *C* at `-O2` changes its payload too, so the\n  payload is outside the ABI contract.\n* **Trap parity** for inputs that fault in the C: run in a child process against\n  each `.so` and compare exit status.\n* Struct outputs are compared **byte-wise**, and every out-parameter is poisoned\n  before the call, so \"did not write\" is distinguishable from \"wrote zero\".\n\n## Defects found and fixed\n\nSymbols matched and the happy path passed from the start; all three real bugs\nwere found by the error-surface and configuration-surface work.\n\n### 1. Bounds checks turned non-trapping C reads into `abort()` — `c2GJK`\n\n`c2GJKCache::count`, `iA[]` and `iB[]` are caller-supplied and **never validated\nby the C**. The translation indexed Rust slices with them\n(`pA.verts[iA as usize]`, `(*cache).iA[i as usize]`, `s.verts[s.count as usize]`,\n`saveA[i as usize]`). With `cache->iA[0] = 8`:\n\n* C returned `dist=inf` and kept running;\n* Rust panicked — *\"index out of bounds: the len is 8 but the index is 8\"* — and\n  died of **SIGABRT** (`panic = \"abort\"` in the release profile).\n\nFixed by mirroring the C's pointer arithmetic at every caller-controlled index.\nRegression test `err37_38_39_ub_rows_do_not_trap` now makes 51 200 such calls per\nlibrary with no trap. (ERRORS.md row 38.)\n\n### 2. Frame-layout mismatch on out-of-range `cache->count` — `c2GJK`\n\nWith `count > 3` the C overruns `saveA[3]`/`saveB[3]` and `verts[4…]`. gcc lays\n`int saveA[3], saveB[3]` out contiguously, so `saveA[3]` harmlessly aliases\n`saveB[0]`; two separate Rust arrays instead clobbered a live local (the `verts`\npointer), turning a survivable C call into a Rust **SIGSEGV**. Fixed by\nallocating the two arrays as one padded buffer and backing the simplex with a\npadded allocation. Verified: for `count` 4…8 with `div == 0.0` (where the\ntype-punned index is 0 and the C does *not* trap) both now return exactly\n`dist=0, count=N, iters=0`. (ERRORS.md rows 35b / 36.)\n\n### 3. An optimised-away unconditional dereference — `c2Support`\n\nThe C evaluates `c2Dot(verts[0], d)` **before** the loop guard ever reads\n`count`. When `count <= 0` the value is unused, so LLVM deleted the load in the\nrelease build: `c2Support(NULL, 0, d)` returned `0` quietly while the C\nsegfaulted. Fixed with `core::ptr::read_volatile` to pin the dereference; the\ntrap-parity row now matches (signal 11 on both). (ERRORS.md row 58.)\n\n## Things worth knowing about this library\n\n* **`gjk_cache` has no observable output.** It computes four `c2GJK` results into\n  locals and discards them; the `a9`/`b9` out-parameters are never dereferenced\n  and never written. The tests assert exactly that (poisoned buffers must survive\n  intact on both sides) rather than inventing an output contract.\n* **The cache-validation condition is effectively a no-op.** `!(min_metric <\n  max_metric * 2.0f && metric < -1.0e8f)` — the second conjunct is almost never\n  true, so a *stale* cache is accepted. That is reproduced verbatim, and both\n  sides of it are tested: 4050 accepted stale caches, and the rejection branch\n  forced 1538 times with large-coordinate AABBs (`metric < -1e8`).\n* **The `iter < 20` cap is structurally unreachable.** Every proxy holds at most\n  4 vertices, so the search always exits via one of the three break guards\n  first; a 30 000-case sweep observed a maximum of 3 iterations. The tests assert\n  that C and Rust agree on `*iterations` and that the cap never breaks, and say\n  so explicitly rather than implying the cap was exercised.\n* **`c2L` treats `count == 3` as `default`** (returns `(0,0)`) while `c2Witness`\n  handles it as a real case — an easy asymmetry to \"fix\" by accident. Preserved.\n* **`c2Maxv`/`c2Minv` drop a NaN in `a` but keep one in `b`**, because the C\n  ternary comparison is false for NaN. Preserved and tested explicitly.\n* **`c2MakeProxy` has no `default:` arm**, so an out-of-range `C2_TYPE` leaves\n  the caller's `c2Proxy` byte-for-byte untouched. Verified against proxies\n  pre-filled with six different byte patterns.\n\n## Limits of what can be verified\n\nRows 37–39 of `ERRORS.md` read uninitialised or out-of-bounds stack in the C.\nThe C is **measurably non-deterministic** there — the identical call returned\n`dist=inf` on one run and `dist=0` on another — so no fixed value exists to match\nand value equality is impossible in principle, not merely inconvenient. Those\nrows assert non-trapping behaviour. For `cache->count >= 9` both libraries\ncorrupt their own frames; C traps at `count=12` while Rust survives until\n`count=100`. This is documented rather than papered over.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5757,"cache_read_input_tokens":301939,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5757,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8eef8cc-b596-45c4-9391-2a80d287efb6","uuid":"a4df4b3d-1f50-4c64-aa75-0a378ba8ee0e","timestamp":"2026-08-20T09:58:15.036Z"}
